From 7b5fb5aa5803148799f6684d51c81ef95d55192a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:53:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: the bytes of a Dolby Vision tunnel through
 the display pipe of DCE

On DCN, amdgpu_dm carries the Dolby Vision tunnel (DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB,
linux-9910) through state that it checks: for that plane and stream DC
programs every colour block in bypass or as the identity. On DCE, with the
sequencer of DCE 11 and the blocks of the dce112 and dce120 pools (Polaris,
Vega M, Vega 10, 12 and 20), DC chooses four things by itself that change the
bytes, and amdgpu_dm can set none of them:

- the line buffer at 30 bpp, with the DCP denormalising to 10 bits before it;
- the prescale of the input pixel processor, which scales an 8-bit surface
  by 0x2020 (1 + 1/256) to bring the zero expansion of the memory input to
  full scale. With the line buffer at 30 bpp, or at 24 bpp if it rounds,
  some 8-bit values would not come out as they went in; with the prescale in
  bypass and the line buffer at 24 bpp, every value does, whatever the
  internal precision (by arithmetic: the review of CroqueMr's linux-9925 for
  Vega M, whose two changes of that kind are taken here);
- the graphics input CSC, which DC never programs: it holds whatever the
  VBIOS, or a driver before DC, left there (amdgpu without DC writes it to
  bypass with each LUT, dce_v10_0_crtc_load_lut());
- the output CSC, programmed as the identity matrix in programmable mode,
  not in its bypass.

A stream gets byte_tunnel, which only amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_fill_stream() sets
(the next patch), and with it on DCE:

- resource_build_scaling_params() puts the line buffer at 24 bpp, in the
  branch that DCE takes (the SPL branch is DCN 4 only and is left alone).
  dce_transform_set_pixel_storage_depth() then programs unity
  denormalisation and the clamp at 8 bits, and with DITHER_OPTION_TRUN8,
  truncation to u0.8 without dither;
- dce110_set_input_transfer_func() puts the graphics input path in bypass
  through a new IPP function, dce_ipp_set_byte_passthrough(): prescale,
  legacy input gamma LUT and graphics input CSC (INPUT_CSC_CONTROL, now in
  the register lists of dce112 and dce120 only). The mode of the input CSC is
  read when the tunnel takes the pipe and written back when the pipe leaves
  it, so that a pipe out of the tunnel is programmed as it was before;
- dce110_program_front_end_for_pipe() programs the input and output transfer
  functions whenever the pipe carries or leaves the tunnel, whatever the
  update bits of the plane say: a new plane state on a new stream has none,
  and after a suspend the registers no longer hold what the IPP last
  programmed. Relying on those bits, as linux-9925 of the fork does, the
  prescale could stay as it was, on the way in or on the way out;
- set_default_colors() asks for the hardware default of the output CSC,
  which for sRGB is its bypass.

dc_stream_read_byte_tunnel() reads back, for the kernel log, what decides
the bytes on such a pipe: the prescale, the input gamma LUT, the input CSC
and the mode it was found in, the degamma, the gamut remap, the regamma, the
output CSC, the denormalisation, clamp, truncation and dither of the DCP, the
line buffer, the scaler, and the truncation, dither, clamp, expansion and
encoding of the formatter. It changes nothing.

Without byte_tunnel nothing changes. A host test builds dce_ipp.c,
dce_transform.c, dce_opp.c, dce_mem_input.c, dce_stream_encoder.c,
dce110_hwseq.c, dc_resource.c, dc_hw_sequencer.c and the dce112 and dce120
pools as they are, on a fake register file. Seven ordinary scenarios (8-bit,
10-bit, RGB565 and FP16 surfaces, the legacy LUT, scaling, limited range,
YCbCr output, dither), each with and without update bits, on DCE 11.2, 11.22,
12.0 and 12.1, make the same 8056 register writes, in the same order and from
the same functions, before and after this patch. With the tunnel, the
register image differs from the same stream without it in eight registers
only (prescale control, input CSC, output CSC, line buffer format,
denormalisation and the three clamps); it is the same again once the tunnel
ends, also straight from a desktop with the legacy LUT and after a suspend;
the Dolby VSIF of 27 bytes is in generic packet 0, its last byte included.
30 mutations of the two patches make the test fail.

Not verified: that a DCE does what the code and the arithmetic say. The
read back in the kernel log is there for the first test on a Polaris, a
Vega M or a Vega with a Dolby Vision TV.
